     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Two things need sorting out first.\n\n**1. The dose looks unsafe.**\n- Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day.\n- The usual maximum for adults is about 2,000\u20132,550 mg/day, depending on the formulation. Single doses are typically 500\u20131,000 mg, and 850 mg is the largest common tablet strength.\n- An overdose of metformin raises the risk of lactic acidosis, which can be life-threatening.\n- This may be a typo. Examples are 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day) and 1,700 mg total per day, often split as 850 mg twice daily.\n\n**2. You're not a clinician.**\nMedication orders should come from, or be verified by, a licensed prescriber. I can't confirm that this order is clinically intended or authorized.\n\n**Next steps**\n- Ask the prescriber to confirm the intended dose and frequency.\n- Once you have a confirmed, clinically reasonable order, send it to me and I'll enter it. A prescriber could also enter it directly.\n\nIf the prescriber really does intend 1700 mg three times daily, I'd want that confirmed explicitly, with a note on the order, before I enter it."
      }
     ],
